WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:04.520 --> 00:00:05.520
This is me.

00:00:05.520 --> 00:00:11.353
And I’m about to enter a building that was
the future of work in 1939.

00:00:11.353 --> 00:00:13.820
First I tripped a bit.

00:00:13.820 --> 00:00:15.160
This Frank Lloyd Wright Building —

00:00:15.160 --> 00:00:19.880
It got a lot right about what the office
would be like and how it would feel

00:00:19.880 --> 00:00:22.373
for the rest of the 20th century.

00:00:23.573 --> 00:00:27.607
And I wanted to try working in the place
where it that started.

00:00:31.544 --> 00:00:34.842
I first learned about this office 
5 years ago.

00:00:34.842 --> 00:00:37.905
Most open offices were designed by consultants.

00:00:37.905 --> 00:00:40.409
This one was designed by a genius.

00:00:44.745 --> 00:00:49.976
It was built as the new headquarters
for SC Johnson in Racine, Wisconsin.

00:00:54.084 --> 00:00:59.211
It came in an era when often offices were
small and cramped or private.

00:00:59.695 --> 00:01:02.643
This building had a spacious 
central room instead

00:01:02.643 --> 00:01:05.406
meant to encourage the spread of ideas.

00:01:05.406 --> 00:01:07.740
Architectural Forum was so excited

00:01:07.740 --> 00:01:12.000
they did a special spread on just the plans
for this building.

00:01:12.000 --> 00:01:15.623
Today, SC Johnson makes stuff like
Glade, Pledge, Ziploc

00:01:15.623 --> 00:01:17.700
Duck, Canard, Pato.

00:01:17.700 --> 00:01:19.638
Those are all the same thing...

00:01:19.638 --> 00:01:20.905
duck.

00:01:21.452 --> 00:01:22.316
Yea.

00:01:23.369 --> 00:01:30.724
But in 1939, it was basically a “Wax company”
that wanted more for its managerial class.

00:01:30.724 --> 00:01:34.643
They hired Frank LLoyd Wright to design it
and he aimed for

00:01:34.643 --> 00:01:40.549
“as inspiring a place to work in as any
cathedral ever was to worship in.”

00:01:40.549 --> 00:01:45.350
They still do tours and most of the employees
have moved to other buildings.

00:01:45.350 --> 00:01:49.447
So that meant, I'd be able to get a desk there.

00:01:50.163 --> 00:01:55.502
I have worked remote for four years,
making this face.

00:01:55.502 --> 00:01:58.130
My coworkers are a list of names I click on.

00:01:58.130 --> 00:02:01.799
I work next to a Pack N' Play.

00:02:02.599 --> 00:02:04.637
So, I wanted to try something different.

00:02:06.785 --> 00:02:10.209
The most striking feature of the
administrative building is a central room:

00:02:10.209 --> 00:02:11.712
the Great Workroom.

00:02:11.712 --> 00:02:14.419
Its main feature is the columns.

00:02:14.419 --> 00:02:18.688
Architect Jonathan Lipman wrote a book
about the SC Johnson headquarters.

00:02:18.688 --> 00:02:25.220
If I'm going on, sort of, a scavenger hunt
of interesting features in that room

00:02:25.220 --> 00:02:26.921
what should I be looking for?

00:02:26.921 --> 00:02:29.120
When people enter this room

00:02:29.120 --> 00:02:32.408
it is very common that they're
kind of gobsmacked.

00:02:34.703 --> 00:02:40.642
The most common metaphor for this room
is that it is subaqueous

00:02:40.642 --> 00:02:47.250
that is that you feel like you're underwater,
you're in maybe a lily pond.

00:02:47.250 --> 00:02:49.133
And what contributes to that?

00:02:49.133 --> 00:02:53.665
Well, clearly, the columns — the famous
columns.

00:02:54.401 --> 00:02:59.181
A key principle across Wright’s work is
compression and expansion.

00:02:59.181 --> 00:03:04.241
The idea of entering a tight space and then
exploding into an open one.

00:03:04.241 --> 00:03:08.363
These columns are what make the expansion
possible in this room.

00:03:11.794 --> 00:03:14.690
Wright called these “Dendriform” columns.

00:03:14.690 --> 00:03:16.142
That means “tree shaped”.

00:03:16.142 --> 00:03:20.590
And he said that each part had a
calyx, stem, and petal.

00:03:20.590 --> 00:03:22.570
18.5 feet at the petal

00:03:22.570 --> 00:03:26.318
they have just a 9 inch diameter
at the base.

00:03:26.318 --> 00:03:31.230
They are so skinny that Wright and SC Johnson
had to prove they were safe

00:03:31.230 --> 00:03:35.232
by piling 12 tons of rocks on top.

00:03:35.232 --> 00:03:40.590
And they did it until they hit 60 tons of
rocks and stopped the test.

00:03:41.222 --> 00:03:45.383
What did it feel like to be surrounded by
these stalks of columns?

00:03:45.383 --> 00:03:47.782
And have this light coming down from above.

00:03:47.782 --> 00:03:49.064
So I went there.

00:03:49.927 --> 00:03:52.410
I called my editor, Bridgett, from the airport.

00:03:53.104 --> 00:03:54.950
It's really hot out here.

00:03:54.950 --> 00:03:56.350
It's like 95 degrees.

00:03:56.350 --> 00:03:57.424
Phil's out in the world.

00:03:57.424 --> 00:03:59.098
Got to get the editor to weigh in.

00:03:59.098 --> 00:04:00.266
We don't use Google Docs.

00:04:00.266 --> 00:04:01.455
We just...

00:04:01.455 --> 00:04:03.718
We always weigh in with one-on-ones.

00:04:03.718 --> 00:04:05.050
Yeah, constant one-on-ones.

00:04:05.050 --> 00:04:06.618
Constant FaceTime.

00:04:06.618 --> 00:04:09.010
Do you have any final thoughts?

00:04:09.010 --> 00:04:11.200
What I should be looking for on my trip?

00:04:11.200 --> 00:04:16.750
Yeah, I mean, the main thing I think that
you should do is actually work there.

00:04:16.750 --> 00:04:19.333
Like, I think that's important, like—

00:04:21.691 --> 00:04:23.779
Oh, no. Is that me or you?

00:04:24.203 --> 00:04:24.921
Phil!

00:04:26.100 --> 00:04:27.095
It overheated.

00:04:27.095 --> 00:04:28.190
Oh no!

00:04:28.190 --> 00:04:29.337
My phone overheated.

00:04:29.337 --> 00:04:31.310
Get inside. Get to air conditioning.

00:04:40.162 --> 00:04:44.383
But before I went to SC Johnson to work,
I wanted a couple more tips.

00:04:44.383 --> 00:04:47.225
So I met up with Mark Hertzberg

00:04:47.225 --> 00:04:51.370
who’s a photojournalist, a Frank Lloyd Wright
expert, and author of a book

00:04:51.370 --> 00:04:54.433
about this Frank Lloyd Wright house,
the Hardy House.

00:04:54.433 --> 00:04:56.680
It came years before SC Johnson Wax

00:04:56.680 --> 00:05:00.431
but you can see some of the same ideas 
at play.

00:05:00.431 --> 00:05:03.270
You enter in this really tight hallway

00:05:03.270 --> 00:05:06.814
which then blows out into the living room
and lake view.

00:05:06.814 --> 00:05:09.231
Compression and expansion.

00:05:20.302 --> 00:05:24.900
There are all these custom furniture features
too, which, well - just remember these.

00:05:25.405 --> 00:05:27.563
Okay, so, Wright liked built-ins

00:05:27.563 --> 00:05:32.469
and one of the magic features of a house
for a little girl, like Anne Ruetz

00:05:32.469 --> 00:05:38.134
growing up in the house when you're
5 years old in the two south bedrooms...

00:05:39.355 --> 00:05:42.270
Wright has these chairs that come out.

00:05:42.270 --> 00:05:44.082
Wow, that is so cool!

00:05:44.082 --> 00:05:47.900
This house made every shot look cool
which was great for me.

00:05:47.900 --> 00:05:51.810
Like every frame just ends up looking cool.

00:05:51.810 --> 00:05:55.809
Like, you look just cool right now, you know?

00:05:55.809 --> 00:05:57.090
I am cool.

00:05:57.090 --> 00:05:59.669
Yeah, it's your natural cool, of course, primarily.

00:05:59.669 --> 00:06:02.527
But, you know, it's just like you've got all
these leading lines right here.

00:06:02.527 --> 00:06:06.919
Well, you know what, that is an
interesting point

00:06:06.919 --> 00:06:13.129
because one of the points I make
to guests when we do tours

00:06:13.129 --> 00:06:17.240
is it if you stand here in the bedroom
this door frame

00:06:17.240 --> 00:06:22.003
frames the door frame to the bedroom
at the opposite end of the balcony.

00:06:22.003 --> 00:06:28.082
And you also see the wood trim that's in the
living room helping frame everything.

00:06:28.082 --> 00:06:32.523
And Mark actually already got to work 
in the SC Johnson Great Workroom.

00:06:32.523 --> 00:06:33.870
Like I wanted to.

00:06:33.870 --> 00:06:37.692
It was a privilege to be allowed to sit in
the Great Workroom

00:06:37.692 --> 00:06:43.308
and to sit at the desk in one of 
the Wright chairs

00:06:43.308 --> 00:06:45.389
and just to be able to look around.

00:06:45.389 --> 00:06:48.210
It was a building I'd been in many times.

00:06:48.210 --> 00:06:50.554
I'd photographed it many times.

00:06:50.554 --> 00:06:53.229
But I had no cameras with me.

00:06:53.861 --> 00:07:01.845
It's just me and my laptop and just to look
around and to drink in the marvels of the design.

00:07:04.561 --> 00:07:08.309
On the left is the research tower,
opened in 1950,

00:07:08.309 --> 00:07:11.253
while at the right you have the Great Workroom.

00:07:11.253 --> 00:07:15.830
You enter through a carport to get there,
very modern for 1939.

00:07:15.830 --> 00:07:17.360
Compression.

00:07:17.360 --> 00:07:21.432
You go past Frank’s signature,
into the lobby, and then...

00:07:21.432 --> 00:07:22.629
expansion.

00:07:28.965 --> 00:07:33.590
SC Johnson’s archivist, Terri Boessl
showed me around.

00:07:33.590 --> 00:07:35.820
There was no reason to do this.

00:07:35.820 --> 00:07:40.199
And yet you had company leadership at the
time who who just really felt

00:07:40.199 --> 00:07:44.419
the need to inspire his employees
and have something here

00:07:44.419 --> 00:07:47.328
in the middle of industrialized Racine.

00:07:48.213 --> 00:07:50.591
We rode in the gold elevator at one point.

00:07:50.591 --> 00:07:52.647
It was very gold.

00:07:55.090 --> 00:07:57.828
Then I put my hands around the column.

00:07:57.828 --> 00:08:02.229
People kept telling me about all the cool
stuff that had happened, but it was only stuff

00:08:02.229 --> 00:08:06.193
that you could see when the building 
was full of people.

00:08:06.193 --> 00:08:10.265
People were collected in one space
without walls.

00:08:10.265 --> 00:08:13.559
Managers perched on the mezzanine
but still open.

00:08:13.559 --> 00:08:17.969
The chairs were originally elegant,
three-legged inventions by Wright

00:08:17.969 --> 00:08:20.710
but they tipped over when people arrived.

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:24.619
He wanted this three-legged design
which it looks good

00:08:24.619 --> 00:08:29.027
and it fits with his really streamlined look.

00:08:29.027 --> 00:08:32.979
But as they told him: it's very unstable.

00:08:32.979 --> 00:08:33.705
We're gonna lean

00:08:33.705 --> 00:08:37.700
we're gonna reach for something,
and the second we do

00:08:37.700 --> 00:08:39.090
the chair's gonna tip over.

00:08:39.090 --> 00:08:41.230
And beautiful ceiling?

00:08:41.230 --> 00:08:42.820
It had flaws too.

00:08:42.820 --> 00:08:45.440
For a long time, we had something called
the bucket brigade

00:08:45.440 --> 00:08:49.590
which was just — the people working in the
space would get the bucket out from under

00:08:49.590 --> 00:08:52.430
their desk and put it where the leak 
comes from.

00:08:52.430 --> 00:08:54.640
But there was also falling glass.

00:08:54.640 --> 00:08:59.300
You know, different pieces of the Pyrex tubing
would fall down.

00:08:59.300 --> 00:09:02.343
This building, it was designed 
before its time.

00:09:02.343 --> 00:09:08.315
And we saw the custom desks, just like the
ones that I had seen in the Hardy House.

00:09:08.315 --> 00:09:12.720
So instead of pulling out a desk drawer, and
the rooting through it to get to the back

00:09:12.720 --> 00:09:15.870
you'd pull it out and you'd have 
the whole thing.

00:09:21.639 --> 00:09:22.536
Wowwww!

00:09:22.536 --> 00:09:24.108
Does it look pretty epic?

00:09:24.108 --> 00:09:25.050
I mean...

00:09:25.050 --> 00:09:27.197
I got to say, I don't want to sound...

00:09:27.892 --> 00:09:28.640
mean...

00:09:28.640 --> 00:09:31.020
but it kind of looks like a hotel lobby
from this angle.

00:09:31.020 --> 00:09:34.283
A very cool hotel lobby.

00:09:34.283 --> 00:09:36.270
I'm only seeing a small bit of it.

00:09:36.270 --> 00:09:40.200
I love the visitor lanyard, it's quite a look.

00:09:40.200 --> 00:09:41.960
It's pretty legit. 

00:09:41.960 --> 00:09:44.940
I think I have to give it back unfortunately.

00:09:44.940 --> 00:09:49.180
I can't really tell because of the way the
exposure is, but is that just cool lights

00:09:49.180 --> 00:09:51.175
up there, that are looking...

00:09:51.175 --> 00:09:56.535
Yeah, so up there is kind of natural light
augmented with artificial light,

00:09:56.535 --> 00:10:04.200
And initially they didn't have enough light,
so they had to add artificial light in there.

00:10:04.200 --> 00:10:08.330
And then people got artificial lights at their
desks and stuff, which Frank Lloyd Wright

00:10:08.330 --> 00:10:09.950
was like mad about.

00:10:09.950 --> 00:10:11.690
It's pretty cool.

00:10:11.690 --> 00:10:15.790
And there's these crazy spiral staircases,
too, that are kind of scary.

00:10:16.295 --> 00:10:18.690
Thank you for talking with me about this.

00:10:18.690 --> 00:10:19.790
Well good luck!

00:10:19.790 --> 00:10:20.790
Bye!

00:10:20.790 --> 00:10:22.040
Okay, bye!

00:10:22.040 --> 00:10:24.020
Then, it was time.

00:10:24.462 --> 00:10:26.012
I'd sit down.

00:10:26.012 --> 00:10:27.710
I’d take a half hour.

00:10:27.710 --> 00:10:28.864
And I’d work.

00:10:32.380 --> 00:10:34.179
Should we establish that you're not Bridgett?

00:10:34.179 --> 00:10:39.064
We should probably establish that I'm not
Bridgett, because it is something that

00:10:39.064 --> 00:10:40.691
people get confused about a lot.

00:10:40.691 --> 00:10:43.632
In person, usually.

00:10:43.632 --> 00:10:45.327
So, Bridgett's on vacation.

00:10:45.327 --> 00:10:48.000
And you are editing the rest of this story.

00:10:48.000 --> 00:10:54.500
So the point in this story, where we are right
now, is that I am sitting down at my desk.

00:10:54.500 --> 00:10:57.360
I really did think that I was going to
work there for half an hour.

00:10:57.360 --> 00:10:59.140
Yeah, you know, the whole point of the video.

00:10:59.140 --> 00:11:00.641
That's the whole gimmick!

00:11:01.062 --> 00:11:03.420
Yeah. It's the whole gimmick.

00:11:03.420 --> 00:11:04.864
Yeah, exactly.

00:11:04.864 --> 00:11:07.998
And then I sat down there and I was like...

00:11:08.988 --> 00:11:10.764
I'm just at a laptop.

00:11:10.764 --> 00:11:12.449
This is just a laptop.

00:11:13.628 --> 00:11:19.096
This is not different from my laptop at home
or my laptop in a coffee shop

00:11:19.096 --> 00:11:21.570
 or anywhere else.

00:11:21.570 --> 00:11:23.810
Like, sitting at a laptop is just sitting
at a laptop.

00:11:24.463 --> 00:11:27.918
This is supposedly the greatest office 
in the world 

00:11:27.918 --> 00:11:32.180
and this is how you've been building this up,
this is the greatest office.

00:11:32.180 --> 00:11:34.016
The greatest place to work.

00:11:34.016 --> 00:11:35.146
And... 

00:11:35.146 --> 00:11:36.893
you couldn't do it for a minute.

00:11:41.019 --> 00:11:44.940
When Herbert Johnson built SC Johnson’s
corporate headquarters

00:11:44.940 --> 00:11:49.348
having a big showpiece headquarters 
for office workers was rare.

00:11:49.348 --> 00:11:53.340
In 1939, this was the future of work.

00:11:53.340 --> 00:11:57.173
This building, it’s the beginning of these
big corporate campuses.

00:11:57.173 --> 00:12:03.587
The Googles and Metas and Amazons 
owe a debt to campus here.

00:12:03.587 --> 00:12:07.356
Wright later wrote that his building 
was the future.

00:12:07.356 --> 00:12:12.642
"It was high time to give our hungry American
public something truly 'streamlined'."

00:12:12.642 --> 00:12:16.080
"That anybody could see the virtue of this
thing called 'Modern.'“

00:12:17.764 --> 00:12:18.668
Hi.

00:12:18.668 --> 00:12:21.328
I live in the future called 2022.

00:12:25.370 --> 00:12:31.218
I am in an office where I can video chat with
a few clicks, across the world.

00:12:31.218 --> 00:12:34.140
And still be by myself.

00:12:34.140 --> 00:12:37.120
Is that expansion or compression?

00:12:37.120 --> 00:12:43.516
It wasn't a real thing without any people
around, without the energy of

00:12:43.516 --> 00:12:44.689
being in that office.

00:12:44.689 --> 00:12:47.510
Then I think, this space would've mattered.

00:12:47.510 --> 00:12:48.938
But I didn't have that.

00:12:48.938 --> 00:12:52.142
So in the end, the real SC Johnson was the
friends we made along the way.

00:12:52.142 --> 00:12:53.740
That's exactly...

00:12:53.740 --> 00:12:56.130
I was so desperate to find out
what it would feel like

00:12:56.130 --> 00:12:57.766
to work in this room.

00:12:57.766 --> 00:12:59.950
With the beautiful dendriform columns

00:12:59.950 --> 00:13:02.050
and incredible colors...

00:13:02.050 --> 00:13:03.632
But without people...

00:13:04.790 --> 00:13:06.327
It’s just a background.

